Hopkins  you know Mayo may be an exception but  that doesn't count it's not a fair  population so we started looking at  Durham County and lo and behold in  Durham County we're only better than the  rest of North Carolina in one statistic  we have twice as many doctors for a  population but if we look at our  world-famous heart center our  cardiovascular mortality is only  minimally better than the state of North  Carolina North Carolina is in the bottom  25% of the United States  I only take solace in the fact that the  Cleveland Clinic the number-one heart  center in the United States is in  Cuyahoga County Cuyahoga County has 2.5  times the cardiovascular mortality rate  as Durham County so it may be the case  that the more renowned your heart center  is the more you ought to stay away from  it if you don't want to die of heart  disease and so we set about to try to  deal with this issue stimulated by the  fact that we haven't a legal monopoly in  the county that we live in it's only two  hundred sixty thousand people but when  we took over the county measure of the  County Hospital between us and the  Public Health Department about  ninety-five percent of the health care  transactions in our County occur either  in it with a Duke provider or a public  health provider in the county it turned  out our school of environment had gotten  excited about geospatial mapping for  environmental reasons and developed  really elegant geospatially referenced  depictions of every household in Durham  County with all the publicly available  information and they initially use this  for a public health use but I think when  you when you see this at me  you can tell you what the use in any  kind of health care might be the issue  was we had a lot of pipes of lead in the  pipes you could either go to every  household in Durham County or you could  stratify the risk per household also  knowing other publicly available  information like where the babies had  been born and you could go to the  highest risk households intensively and  leave the professor's alone because it's  likely that we're going to take care of  our own pipes and that's what was done  it was highly successful but this gave  us a generalizable lesson taking this  lesson and remembering this is right  after the la crosse case which is still  not over they're still lawsuits going on  and in a county where there was both a  town-gown problem and also a major  history of racial strife related to the  part of the country we're in with Durham  County haven't been one of the major  places where civil rights were taken  very seriously in the 1960s so we  engaged in a massive effort to get the  whole population involved in this and  the premise that we had was you're gonna  be better off if we know everything  about you and you know everything about  yourself when here we're talking about  your health data we're talking about  your financial data we're talking about  your crimes we're talking about your  grocery stores your churches your  schools you name it and so we developed  a system where we've now integrated the  Ducal system data the public health data  the publicly available data about the  environment and all of the other  information that you can get into one  system and I'm just going to show you  the application that we've developed for  diabetes and obesity and and the  implementation of this is just starting  this year because it took two years of  almost pure community engagement to get  to the point where we were confident we  could go forward and by the way the  response in the neighborhoods has been  amazing  we've had really no opposition to this  and their general response has been it's  about time  that's what you knew about us very  interesting response so if we look at  diabetes in Durham County I'm sure it  all came through here on the yes there  it is on the slide this is obviously a  problem which is more in women than men  and by the way one reason for this  that's it's predominantly an  african-american problem in our County  40% of our population is  african-american a lot of the men are  dead before they have a chance to get  diabetes and that's part of the problem  that we're dealing with but it's also a  disease of people are underinsured and  undereducated if we look at the  demographics of Durham County we can  show neighborhood by neighborhood quite  a bit of variation this is no surprise  to anyone in population density in  median income and n percent African  American and we can slice and dice this  in any way that you want but it  shouldn't surprise you if you knew  exactly where a Duke University was Duke  University it's right in this area we  are surrounded by populations with lower  income and lower educational status as  many universities are and this is a look  then at each individual in Durham County  now there's a method use here to  randomly vary where these dots are so  this is actually not exactly or each  individual lives but we have permission  to use the information on where each  individual lives both for deployment of  healthcare and for research so each dot  here represents an individual person  this is a look at all the people in  Durham County to have diabetes and you  can see they're not evenly distributed  around the county and there are a number  of statistics about them that we have  both in terms of their health and in  terms of other things about them now  interesting like 24 percent of these  people have not had a hemoglobin a1c in  the Duke Health System record in the  last year now I didn't dwell on it but  on the first slide I showed you about  diving  the average number of times these people  touch the Ducal system is 13 but they're  doing a lot of different things and they  may have had a hemoglobin a1c measured  over at UNC which is only 80 miles away  but you would think that if you were  being seen 13 times in a Health System  somebody would have put the human leaven  anyone see in a place where it's  accessible in the medical record and  this is a look at the 16 percent of  people who have hemoglobin a1c is  greater than 9 or the whole spectrum but  I'm interested in a 16% and exactly  where they live and who they are now  when you begin to see this and put this  in the context of the lead poisoning  example it begins to make you think  about how stupid we are to build clinics  and tell people come to our clinics when  we actually know exactly where they live  and know that they're not coming to  clinics on their own now this is taking  a look at central Durham where of course  our focus is because that's where the  problem is you can see the density of  people here you have diabetes and and  also broken down here by  african-american status and by age  because obviously your intervention for  example in a 40 aryl african-american on  average might be quite different than a  60 year old English professor at the  University and so what we've done now is  to take this information and together  with the community ask the question how  can we best deploy our troops our health  services troops to have the biggest  impact on the outcomes in the county of  Durham and needless to say it is not by  building clinics at Duke hospital in  fact I used to go to two meetings one on  Wednesday mornings was the executive  committee of the Health System we use  data like this to decide where to put  our clinics and you can imagine where we  were putting them it's where you make  the most money and then in the afternoon  we had disparities meeting we could see  our clinic should go if you want to do  improve health which was exactly the  opposite of where they were going the  beauty of this for us is because of the  fact that  have a legal monopoly it's in our  interest to actually improve health  financially and we've done the  calculations on this so they were not  going against our financial interests as  we do this just to show you the things  that we're using in deployment this is  just a look at the social services the  clinics the churches the grocery stores  the beauty salons and the barber shops  now most specialists don't think about  barbershops and beauty salons but it  actually turns out as they've shown in  Dallas and among african-american men  the social institution where the  greatest loyalty is the barbershop on  average and so teaching barbers to take  blood pressure is a critical part of a  community strategy well we've also done  this together with the American Heart  Association is to deploy kiosks that  measure blood pressure and download the  information directly into the electronic  record in the high-risk neighborhoods  among with the professor's like me I was  actually the first participant in the  hypertension part of this because I have  hypertension I just got a blood pressure  cuff and I downloaded my information  myself over the Internet but there are  no doctors involved in this part of the  intervention except for the refractory  cases because nurse nurses and nurse  practitioners can handle their  prescriptions and then finally you might  say what about the divine and the  continuity between the divide really if  you're deploying things correctly or at  least this is our theory they said once  you have data like these you readily see  that most of the work needs to be done  in the neighborhoods where people live  but there will still be plenty of work  left over for doctors and Clinics it  will just be more of the appropriate  work that needs to be done and we've  still got a lot of work to do there and  it's a joining of these in a common  informatics system that really can make  the difference so I'm not going to  identify which one is me but if we just  look at hypertension I'm looking at  cardiology clinics you can see that even  among cardiologists we ain't to go to  control and hypertension and so it's not  just that we're out in the neighborhoods  we think we have to bring this whole  continuous spectrum from where someone  lives all the way to the clinic in the  hospital  into a common system that can be  monitored as we go through it so this is  the last slide it just sort of gives the  scheme that we're using we start with a  data architecture and interestingly in  the community of Durham after this after  six months of continuous going at it  with people in the community their  number one desire to was to have a  single electronic record that all  providers could use including social  agencies so that we wouldn't be so  confused about other treatments that  people are getting and so that we could  integrate our care and we've identified  areas that are purely clinical care in  the traditional sense in areas that are  purely the community and then what we  call a partnership zone where we work  together continuously but just like  Harlan has shown in clinics and many  people are shown in hospitals we now  think we can use a demographic unit as a  continuous quality improvement vehicle  with constant monitoring every time a  person hits any part of the health  system or the community system that  information goes into the system it's  integrated and is available to everyone  else in the system so that we can  measure the health of the community as a  continuum not as something that we ask  say every year every five years as has  been done in the past in public health  so I hope what I've given you is it's a  view for where things could go this is  based on a view that a lot of this is  going to be driven by the fact that  information is going to be so free that  even if we'd like to huddle in our  little clinics in hospitals  cloistered in our fortresses we're not  going to be able to do it because the  demand is going to be there for us to  deal with information and where people  live and give help them to get what they  need in order to live healthier lives so  thanks for your attention I hope that's  been a good way to end this symposium
 

